content.text:
Two things are moving: the doctor fix is back in CI after I corrected its test, and PF-2 is under way with a subagent.

- **Doctor fix (#704):** the first CI run failed on my own new test, which relied on the scan running out of time. The CI runner read all 40,000 test files inside the time budget, so no partial-scan warning appeared. The product fix was fine. I made the scan's entry limit settable (`NEXUS_DOCTOR_STATE_SCAN_ENTRIES`) and changed the test to stop the scan by count, so it no longer depends on disk speed. It passes three runs in a row locally, and CI is rerunning.
- **PF-2 (`nexus run --json` contract):** I checked the claims first this time. The files were free, so I claimed them and handed the work to a subagent:
  - a `schema_version` on the result;
  - a generated, checked-in schema;
  - one documented table of exit codes;
  - reading the task from `--input-json` or stdin.

  It is told not to touch the protocol files Codex now owns.

I also added a note to my claim files saying this session's process IDs always look absent, since each of my commands runs in its own short-lived shell. That appears to be why the earlier takeover recorded my session as gone.

